  "semantic_decisions": [
    {
      "clause": "node200 time scaling",
      "approved": true,
      "reason": "A differentiable constant timing s=0 has nonnegative derivative while its path image is only q(0). Nonnegative derivative preserves parameter order on the differentiable time interval; coverage of the full [0,1] parameter interval supplies equality of images. Derivative zero is stated only as instantaneous stopping. Displayed first/second derivative formulas and oscillator arithmetic are unchanged."
    },
    {
      "clause": "node202 backprop domain",
      "approved": true,
      "reason": "The ordinary activation chain rule applies when every scalar activation is differentiable at its preactivation. The printed example has both preactivations positive, so its v=(1.5,.5), gradient=(2,0), and local f=2x1 calculation are unchanged."
    },
    {
      "clause": "node202 possible output nondifferentiability",
      "approved": true,
      "reason": "Finite ReLU compositions are continuous piecewise affine. A zero/nonzero sign boundary for a nonconstant affine preactivation is a proper hyperplane piece; identically zero tests create no switching face. Output nondifferentiability can occur only on finitely many such pieces, which have Lebesgue measure zero. Identical neurons with output weights1,-1 cancel to the differentiable zero function, so no universal nondifferentiability claim remains. An autodiff convention at zero remains explicitly a convention."
    }
  ],
